  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The most effective solution is to use <b>Leverage a Docker image that includes the legacy programming languages and all required dependencies, then deploy this containerized application on Elastic Beanstalk</b>. By utilizing Docker, you can package the entire application environment—including specific programming languages or libraries that are not provided directly by Elastic Beanstalk—into a portable and repeatable container. Elastic Beanstalk has robust support for deploying and managing Docker containers, so this strategy overcomes limitations with official platform support.</p><p>The option <i>Set up an AWS OpsWorks stack to provision the legacy programming languages and application dependencies</i> is not suitable as AWS OpsWorks is a separate configuration management service; it does not allow you to run an application directly on Elastic Beanstalk, nor does it directly integrate with Beanstalk environments.</p><p>The option <i>Develop a custom Elastic Beanstalk platform, ensuring it contains the needed legacy languages and application dependencies</i> could potentially work but usually involves significant effort and maintenance to build, update, and support your own custom EB platform. Docker provides a much simpler and more standardized approach.</p><p>The option <i>Build an Elastic Beanstalk environment utilizing AWS OpsWorks, and deploy the application stack to Elastic Beanstalk using OpsWorks</i> is incorrect because there is no supported way to directly deploy an OpsWorks stack to an Elastic Beanstalk environment; the services are independent with different purposes.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> When standard Elastic Beanstalk platforms do not fulfill your application requirements—especially due to custom or legacy dependencies—containerizing your application with Docker is considered a best practice on AWS, offering maximum compatibility and portability.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your software development team wishes to move their current application into AWS Elastic Beanstalk. However, the app depends on outdated programming languages and a set of legacy dependencies that the standard Elastic Beanstalk environment does not natively support. Which strategy best enables you to deploy the app on Elastic Beanstalk while fulfilling these requirements?</p>",
